  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">A comprehensive neuroanatomical survey of the Drosophila Lobula Plate Tangential Neurons with predictions for their optic flow sensitivity (2024) in eLife. 2 citations.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">A comprehensive neuroanatomical survey of the Drosophila Lobula Plate Tangential Neurons with predictions for their optic flow sensitivity (2024) in eLife is authored by Arthur Zhao, Aljoscha Nern and Sanna Koskela. Topics: Neuroscience, Biology, Sensory system, Drosophila (subgenus) and Receptive field.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">2 citations (OpenAlex). Citations peaked in 2024 with 1 citing works. Citing venues include bioRxiv (Cold Spring Harbor Laboratory) and eLife.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Limited online visibility beyond citations.</p>",
  "provenance": {},
  "story_type": "ArticleStory",
  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This 2024 eLife article is already showing early scholarly uptake, with 2 citations and 2 documented downstream reuse events. The citing record suggests interest from connectomics and insect neurobiology communities, while the work remains in an early phase of diffusion.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Arthur Zhao and colleagues present a comprehensive neuroanatomical survey of Drosophila lobula plate tangential neurons, extending prior work on optic-flow-sensitive circuits in flying insects. Published in eLife in 2024 and distributed as open access, the article combines anatomical analysis with predictions about how these neurons may respond to optic flow, making it relevant to neuroscience, sensory systems, and insect neurobiology.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The clearest impact signal so far is scholarly rather than public: the work has 2 citations in OpenAlex and has been reused in 2 downstream works, including both preprints and an article. That pattern points to early incorporation into ongoing research rather than broad public discussion. The citation landscape is still small, so the record supports a cautious reading: the paper is beginning to circulate within a focused technical community, but the evidence is still thin.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The citing works cluster around related neuroscience and connectomics topics, including a later article on controlled flight and preprints on visuomotor transformation and connectome analysis. This suggests the paper is being drawn on as part of a broader effort to understand how neural circuits support flight control and visual processing in flies. The downstream record is consistent with reuse by researchers working on mechanistic and computational questions in nervous-system organization.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Other signals remain limited. Scite records one mentioning reference, but there is no documented public event archive activity in the provided record. Taken together, the available evidence indicates a nascent but credible research footprint, with the strongest momentum coming from direct scholarly reuse and citation rather than from wider public attention.</p>",
  "narrative_source": "template"
